// ── Chris Morgan archive (public only, hidden from admin) ─
function getChrisMorganArticles(): array {
    return [
        ['id'=>9100,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>312,'title'=>'The Quiet Crisis in Rural Healthcare','exc'=>'Dozens of small-town hospitals have shut their doors in the past decade, leaving millions without access to emergency care within a reasonable drive.','body'=>'<p>Across the American heartland, a quiet medical emergency has been unfolding for years. Since 2010, more than 140 rural hospitals have permanently closed, and analysts estimate another 450 are at immediate financial risk. For the communities left behind, the consequences are measured in minutes — the minutes it now takes an ambulance to reach the nearest functioning emergency department.</p><p>In Callaway County, Missouri, the closure of the local hospital in 2019 added an average of 34 minutes to emergency response times. Studies published in the Journal of the American Medical Association have consistently found that for time-sensitive conditions such as heart attacks and strokes, each additional ten minutes of transport time is associated with a measurable increase in mortality.</p><p>The drivers of these closures are well understood: declining populations, an older demographic that generates higher costs, an outsized dependence on Medicare and Medicaid reimbursements that consistently pay less than the cost of care, and a shortage of physicians willing to practise in underserved rural areas. What is less well understood is how to reverse the trend.</p>','cat'=>'Science','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Health','Rural','Healthcare'],'date'=>'2021-03-14 09:22:00'],
        ['id'=>9101,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>488,'title'=>'Inside the World\'s Largest Vertical Farm','exc'=>'A converted warehouse in New Jersey now grows enough leafy greens to supply 70 supermarkets — without a single drop of rain.','body'=>'<p>The building looks unremarkable from the outside — a squat, grey warehouse set among a grid of industrial parks in Kearny, New Jersey. Inside, however, is a 70,000-square-foot column of light, nutrient mist, and leafy green life stacked fourteen layers high. This is AeroFarms, one of the largest indoor vertical farms on earth, and it represents a growing bet that the future of food production lies not in fields but in factories.</p><p>Vertical farming uses a fraction of the land and water required by conventional agriculture — AeroFarms claims its system uses 95% less water than field farming — and because the growing environment is entirely controlled, crops can be produced 365 days a year regardless of weather, season, or geography. Yields per square foot are orders of magnitude higher than any outdoor equivalent.</p>','cat'=>'Science','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1558618666-fcd25c85cd64?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Food','Technology','Agriculture'],'date'=>'2019-07-22 14:15:00'],
        ['id'=>9102,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>276,'title'=>'The Rise of Digital Nomad Visas','exc'=>'More than 50 countries now offer special visas for remote workers. We asked people who\'ve used them whether they actually work.','body'=>'<p>When Barbados launched its Welcome Stamp programme in the summer of 2020, it was designed as a pandemic-era lifeline — a way to attract high-earning remote workers to a tourism economy that had been devastated by travel restrictions. The 12-month visa cost $2,000 and promised a year of sun, sea, and stable wi-fi. Within weeks, the government was processing more applications than it had anticipated in an entire year.</p><p>Five years later, Barbados has competition. More than 50 countries now offer some form of digital nomad visa, from Portugal\'s D8 "passive income" visa to Georgia\'s Remotely from Georgia programme, which is free and issued within ten days. The programmes share a common logic: remote workers spend money locally, pay fees, and do not compete for local jobs. What is not to like?</p>','cat'=>'Lifestyle','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1522199755839-a2bacb67c546?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Travel','Remote Work','Lifestyle'],'date'=>'2022-11-05 11:30:00'],
        ['id'=>9103,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>341,'title'=>'Carbon Capture: The Technology That Might Save Us (Or Might Not)','exc'=>'Direct air capture plants are proliferating, but critics question whether the energy cost and price tag make them a viable climate solution.','body'=>'<p>On a windswept lava field in Iceland, a small cluster of industrial fans hums quietly as it draws ambient air across a chemical filter, stripping carbon dioxide molecules from the atmosphere one part per million at a time. The plant, operated by Swiss company Climeworks under the name Orca, became the world\'s first commercial direct air capture facility when it opened in 2021. It can remove 4,000 tonnes of CO₂ per year — a figure that sounds significant until you realise that global emissions run at approximately 37 billion tonnes annually.</p><p>The scale problem is the central criticism of direct air capture as a climate strategy. Removing one tonne of CO₂ from the atmosphere currently costs between $400 and $1,000, depending on the technology and energy source. To have a meaningful impact on atmospheric concentrations, the world would need to be capturing billions of tonnes per year by mid-century.</p>','cat'=>'Science','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1473341304170-971dccb5ac1e?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Climate','Carbon','Environment'],'date'=>'2023-04-18 16:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9104,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>195,'title'=>'What Happened to the Promise of Electric Aviation?','exc'=>'Battery-powered regional aircraft were supposed to be in commercial service by now. Here\'s why they\'re still on the drawing board.','body'=>'<p>In 2019, a consortium of aviation start-ups, established manufacturers, and regional airlines promised that the skies would look different by 2025. Short-haul flights of up to 500 kilometres would be operated by all-electric aircraft, cutting emissions and noise while reducing operating costs by as much as 40%. Five years on, not a single battery-powered commercial passenger aircraft has entered revenue service anywhere in the world.</p><p>The fundamental constraint is energy density. The best lithium-ion cells currently available store roughly 250 watt-hours per kilogram. Jet fuel, by comparison, stores about 12,000 watt-hours per kilogram — nearly 50 times as much. And unlike a car, an aircraft cannot stop and recharge mid-journey, cannot add more batteries without paying an exponential weight penalty, and cannot afford the same margin for error on range estimates.</p>','cat'=>'Technology','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Aviation','Electric','Technology'],'date'=>'2024-02-29 08:45:00'],
        ['id'=>9105,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>427,'title'=>'The Loneliness Epidemic Nobody Talks About','exc'=>'Social isolation is now classified as a public health crisis in several countries. Researchers say the health impact equals smoking 15 cigarettes a day.','body'=>'<p>In 2023, US Surgeon General Vivek Murthy published an advisory — the most serious form of public health guidance his office can issue — declaring loneliness and social isolation a public health epidemic. The document cited research linking chronic loneliness to a 29% increased risk of heart disease, a 32% increased risk of stroke, and a 50% increased risk of dementia. The mortality impact, one widely cited meta-analysis found, is equivalent to smoking 15 cigarettes a day.</p><p>The advisory was notable not because its findings were new — researchers had been publishing on the health consequences of social isolation for decades — but because it elevated a subject often dismissed as a subjective emotional complaint to the level of a clinical emergency. And the numbers justified the urgency. A 2021 survey found that 36% of all Americans, including 61% of young adults, reported feeling seriously lonely.</p>','cat'=>'Lifestyle','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Mental Health','Society','Loneliness'],'date'=>'2023-08-11 10:15:00'],
        ['id'=>9106,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>512,'title'=>'The Shadow Fleet: How Russia Keeps Its Oil Moving','exc'=>'Hundreds of aging tankers with opaque ownership structures are circumventing Western sanctions and reshaping global energy flows.','body'=>'<p>On satellite imagery, they appear as anonymous blobs on open water — oil tankers riding low, clearly laden, moving between Russian ports and receivers in Asia and the Middle East. Many travel with their automatic identification system transponders switched off, rendering them effectively invisible to conventional maritime tracking. These are the ships of what analysts call the shadow fleet, and they are the primary mechanism by which Russia has continued to generate oil revenue despite the most sweeping financial sanctions in modern history.</p><p>The fleet numbers somewhere between 400 and 600 vessels, depending on the methodology used to identify them. Most are old — average age over 15 years — fly flags of convenience from jurisdictions with minimal oversight, and are insured by entities headquartered in countries outside the Western sanctions regime. Many change their names and ownership structures so frequently that they become effectively untraceable.</p>','cat'=>'World News','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1545558014-8692077e9b5c?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Russia','Sanctions','Energy','Shipping'],'date'=>'2024-06-03 13:20:00'],
        ['id'=>9107,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>298,'title'=>'Inside the Comeback of Vinyl Records','exc'=>'Vinyl outsold CDs for the first time in decades last year. What is driving the resurgence of a 1970s technology in the streaming age?','body'=>'<p>The numbers surprised almost everyone in the music industry. In 2022, vinyl records outsold CDs in the United States for the first time since 1987, generating $1.2 billion in revenue against the compact disc\'s $483 million. It was not a fluke — the trend has continued, and analysts now project vinyl to maintain its lead for the foreseeable future even as streaming accounts for more than 80% of all music consumption by revenue.</p><p>The recovery was not driven by nostalgia alone, though that played a role. The key shift has been demographic: the fastest-growing segment of vinyl buyers is not middle-aged collectors reliving their youth but listeners under 35 who are buying their first record players. For this cohort, vinyl represents something streaming cannot offer: an object, an experience, a physical relationship with music.</p>','cat'=>'Entertainment','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1483412033650-1015ddeb83d1?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Music','Vinyl','Entertainment'],'date'=>'2022-09-14 15:40:00'],
        ['id'=>9108,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>363,'title'=>'The Countries Paying Citizens to Have Children','exc'=>'From Hungary\'s baby bonus to Japan\'s $25,000 per child, governments are trying everything to reverse falling birth rates. Almost none of it is working.','body'=>'<p>In 2019, Hungarian Prime Minister Viktor Orbán announced a sweeping package of financial incentives designed to reverse his country\'s population decline. Mothers who had four or more children would be exempt from income tax for life. Newly married couples could receive a subsidised loan of around $35,000, to be forgiven if they had three children. The government would fund IVF treatments, expand subsidised childcare, and offer cash payments at each additional birth.</p><p>Five years later, Hungary\'s birth rate has barely moved. After a brief uptick in 2020 and 2021 that supporters attributed to the policies, the total fertility rate has settled back below 1.6 — well below the 2.1 replacement rate. Similar patterns have emerged in every country that has attempted large-scale pro-natalist intervention: South Korea, Japan, Sweden, and Singapore have all poured billions into incentives with modest and often temporary results.</p>','cat'=>'World News','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Demographics','Policy','Society'],'date'=>'2024-03-07 09:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9109,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>445,'title'=>'The Man Who Photographed Every Street in New York','exc'=>'Over 23 years, he walked and documented all 6,300 miles of New York City streets. We asked him why.','body'=>'<p>Andrew Thompson started photographing the streets of New York in 2001, the year after he moved to the city from rural Pennsylvania. It began as a weekend habit — a camera, a subway MetroCard, and a vague intention to explore the outer boroughs he kept meaning to visit. Twenty-three years and roughly 400,000 photographs later, he has documented every publicly accessible street in all five boroughs, a project that has consumed a substantial fraction of his adult life and produced an archive of extraordinary documentary value.</p><p>The collection is not a gallery project or a commercial venture. Thompson, who works as a tax accountant, has never sold a print and has no plans to do so. The archive lives on three external hard drives in his apartment and on a website that averages about 200 visitors a day. He posts without comment: address, date, direction of shot. The image is the whole of it.</p>','cat'=>'Lifestyle','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1534430480872-3498386e7856?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Photography','New York','Art'],'date'=>'2024-10-02 12:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9110,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>387,'title'=>'How the Panama Canal Became the World\'s Most Contested Waterway','exc'=>'Drought, geopolitics, and the expansion of container shipping have turned a century-old engineering marvel into a flashpoint.','body'=>'<p>For most of its 110-year history, the Panama Canal operated as a reliable piece of infrastructure — a 51-mile shortcut between the Atlantic and Pacific oceans that the shipping industry took essentially for granted. That changed in 2023, when the worst drought in decades reduced water levels in Gatun Lake, the artificial reservoir that fills the canal\'s locks, to their lowest point on record. The canal authority began restricting the number and draft of vessels it would permit daily, triggering a backlog of hundreds of ships and sending freight rates soaring.</p><p>The drought was a forcing event that exposed vulnerabilities that had been building for years. The canal, which handles about 5% of global trade, had become far more critical to supply chains than any single piece of infrastructure arguably should be. When it underperforms, the effects ripple through commodity markets, automotive supply chains, and retail inventories in ways that the original designers of the waterway could not have imagined.</p>','cat'=>'Business','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1558618666-fcd25c85cd64?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Trade','Shipping','Panama','Infrastructure'],'date'=>'2024-01-18 14:30:00'],
        ['id'=>9111,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>221,'title'=>'The Architecture of Solitude: Buildings Designed for Silence','exc'=>'From meditation centres to silent libraries, architects are increasingly asked to design spaces where the aim is to minimise sound and maximise stillness.','body'=>'<p>The brief was unusual even by the standards of a profession accustomed to unusual briefs. A technology executive wanted a residential retreat in the mountains of Colorado that would operate as a kind of monastic enclave — a place for extended stays of solitary work and contemplation, with no connectivity to the outside world and an acoustic environment designed to approach absolute silence. The budget was substantial. The challenge was architectural.</p><p>Silence, it turns out, is extraordinarily difficult to engineer. Human beings can perceive sounds as quiet as zero decibels — the threshold of hearing — and the natural environment, even in supposedly remote locations, is rarely below 20 decibels. Building vibration, HVAC systems, and the acoustic properties of materials all contribute to what engineers call the noise floor: the minimum sound level that persists even when nothing is audibly happening.</p>','cat'=>'Lifestyle','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1545324418-cc1a3fa10c00?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Architecture','Design','Wellness'],'date'=>'2021-06-30 11:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9112,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>334,'title'=>'The Investors Betting Billions on Nuclear Fusion','exc'=>'Private fusion start-ups have raised over $6 billion in the past three years. At least one claims commercial power by 2030. Should we believe them?','body'=>'<p>For seventy years, nuclear fusion has carried a reputation as the energy technology that is always thirty years away. The joke, repeated with weary regularity at physics conferences, reflects a genuine history of optimism repeatedly deferred. The machine always works in principle. The engineering always takes longer than expected. The plasma always finds a new way to escape its magnetic cage at precisely the wrong moment.</p><p>Something appears to be changing. Since 2020, private investors have poured more than $6 billion into fusion start-ups, funding approaches ranging from Commonwealth Fusion Systems\'s high-temperature superconducting tokamak to TAE Technologies\'s beam-driven field-reversed configuration to First Light Fusion\'s projectile-based inertial confinement system. These are not speculative research grants — they are venture capital bets on specific companies with specific timelines and commercial roadmaps.</p>','cat'=>'Science','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1507525428034-b723cf961d3e?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Energy','Fusion','Technology','Investment'],'date'=>'2023-11-27 16:45:00'],
        ['id'=>9113,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>178,'title'=>'Why Young People Are Leaving Major Cities','exc'=>'Remote work gave people options they never had before. A new generation is taking them — and reshaping geography in the process.','body'=>'<p>The pattern has shown up in census data, real estate records, and commuter surveys across every major English-speaking city: since 2020, the outflow of young adults from large metropolitan areas has accelerated sharply, and in several cities it has exceeded the inflow for the first time in a generation. London, New York, San Francisco, and Sydney all saw net population declines among the 25-to-34 demographic in the years following the pandemic, reversing a decades-long trend of urban concentration.</p><p>The proximate cause is well understood. Remote and hybrid working arrangements, which became the norm for a significant portion of knowledge-economy jobs during the pandemic, removed the labour-market penalty that had historically kept young professionals in expensive city centres. If your employer does not require you to be in an office five days a week, the calculus of paying $3,500 a month for a one-bedroom apartment within reasonable commuting distance changes fundamentally.</p>','cat'=>'Lifestyle','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Cities','Remote Work','Demographics'],'date'=>'2022-05-19 09:30:00'],
        ['id'=>9114,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>562,'title'=>'The Chip That Changed Everything: Inside TSMC\'s Dominance','exc'=>'Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company makes the most advanced chips on earth. Its concentration of expertise has become a geopolitical tinderbox.','body'=>'<p>In a cluster of cleanrooms in Hsinchu, Taiwan, workers in full-body suits tend to machines the size of buses that perform operations measured in angstroms — fractions of the diameter of a hydrogen atom. These are the extreme ultraviolet lithography systems made by Dutch company ASML, and they are operated by Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company, the foundry that manufactures roughly 90% of the world\'s most advanced semiconductor chips.</p><p>TSMC\'s dominance is not an accident of geography. It is the product of decades of accumulated process knowledge, capital investment that now runs at $30 billion a year, and an engineering workforce that has become effectively irreplaceable in the medium term. When executives at Apple, Nvidia, AMD, and Qualcomm design a chip, they design it to TSMC\'s process parameters. Their chips are TSMC chips in the most meaningful sense: they would not function if manufactured anywhere else.</p>','cat'=>'Technology','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1518770660439-4636190af475?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Semiconductors','TSMC','Geopolitics','Technology'],'date'=>'2022-08-03 13:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9115,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>289,'title'=>'The Return of Protectionism: What It Means for Consumers','exc'=>'From the US Inflation Reduction Act to the EU\'s Carbon Border Adjustment, governments are erecting new trade walls. Here\'s who pays.','body'=>'<p>For three decades after the end of the Cold War, the dominant paradigm in global economic policy was comparative advantage: countries should produce what they are best at, trade freely with each other, and trust that the aggregate efficiency gains would benefit everyone. The World Trade Organisation was built on this premise. Supply chains stretched across continents and oceans because doing so was cheaper, and cheaper was assumed to be better.</p><p>The pandemic, the war in Ukraine, and the semiconductor shortage that crippled the automotive industry have collectively shattered that confidence. Governments across the political spectrum have moved to onshore production of goods deemed strategically important — semiconductors, batteries, pharmaceuticals, steel, and clean energy equipment — using subsidies, tariffs, local content requirements, and tax credits that would have been considered straightforwardly protectionist under the old consensus.</p>','cat'=>'Business','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Trade','Economics','Policy'],'date'=>'2023-06-22 10:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9116,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>431,'title'=>'The Last Wild River: Racing to Protect the Vjosa','exc'=>'Europe\'s last major undammed river runs through Albania and faces growing pressure from hydropower developers. A coalition of scientists and activists is fighting to save it.','body'=>'<p>The Vjosa rises in the Pindus mountains of northern Greece and flows 272 kilometres northwest through Albania before emptying into the Adriatic. It is Europe\'s last major wild river — meaning a river that runs its full length without a single dam, weir, or significant artificial modification. It has been running more or less as it does now since the Pleistocene, and the ecosystem it supports is of extraordinary ecological value: 1,100 invertebrate species, more than 60 fish species, 11 of which are endemic to the Vjosa system, and nesting habitat for some of the rarest birds in Europe.</p><p>For much of the past decade, that ecosystem has been under threat from a series of proposed hydropower developments that would, if built, transform the Vjosa into a staircase of reservoirs. The projects promised energy and development to one of Europe\'s poorest countries. They also faced fierce opposition from an international coalition of scientists, environmental NGOs, and athletes who argued that the river\'s ecological value far exceeded any benefit from the electricity it might generate.</p>','cat'=>'Science','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1500534314209-a25ddb2bd429?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Environment','Rivers','Conservation','Europe'],'date'=>'2021-09-09 14:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9117,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>215,'title'=>'Inside the Quiet Revival of Print Journalism','exc'=>'While digital news struggles with declining trust and ad revenue, some independent print magazines are reporting their best circulations in years.','body'=>'<p>The conventional wisdom about the future of journalism has been consistent for two decades: print is dying, digital is the future, and the only viable business model is scale. The conventional wisdom has been wrong in interesting ways. While newspaper circulation has indeed declined catastrophically — US daily newspaper circulation fell from 55 million in 2000 to under 20 million today — a different segment of the print market has been quietly doing something unexpected: growing.</p><p>Independent magazines, long-form journals, and niche print publications have reported circulation increases in the years since the pandemic. The New European, a British political weekly launched in 2016 with almost no expectations of longevity, now sells 40,000 copies a week. Delayed Gratification, a slow-journalism quarterly that publishes three months after events, has grown its subscriber base by 30% since 2020. The Drift, a cultural criticism magazine launched in 2021, sold out its first print run and has maintained consistent growth since.</p>','cat'=>'Entertainment','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Journalism','Print','Media'],'date'=>'2023-01-25 09:15:00'],
        ['id'=>9118,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>378,'title'=>'The Psychology of Extreme Risk: Why People Climb Everest','exc'=>'More than 300 people have died on Everest. Thousands more attempt the summit every year. What drives them?','body'=>'<p>In the spring of 2023, Mount Everest saw more summit attempts than any previous year in its recorded history. More than 600 permits were issued by the Nepalese government, generating approximately $5 million in permit fees alone. The mountain was, by any metric, more crowded than ever. It was also more deadly: 17 people died or went missing on the mountain that season, the highest single-year total in a decade.</p><p>The spectacle of people queuing at 8,000 metres, risking their lives in the death zone for a view they could not fully perceive through hypoxia-clouded consciousness, has prompted a recurring question: why? The answer, researchers in the psychology of extreme sports suggest, is more nuanced than the simple invocation of adrenaline or ego that dominates popular explanations.</p>','cat'=>'Lifestyle','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1464822759023-fed622ff2c3b?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Adventure','Psychology','Mountains','Everest'],'date'=>'2023-07-04 11:30:00'],
        ['id'=>9119,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>267,'title'=>'The Countries Disappearing Under Rising Seas','exc'=>'Three Pacific nations have formally purchased land abroad as climate insurance. The concept of a nation without territory raises profound legal questions.','body'=>'<p>In 2023, the government of Tuvalu — a Polynesian nation of eleven coral atolls in the Pacific Ocean — signed an agreement with Australia that included a formal pathway to Tuvaluan citizenship for all of Tuvalu\'s 11,000 citizens. The agreement was described diplomatically as a partnership in resilience. It was also an acknowledgement that the highest point of Tuvalu\'s capital island stands only 4.5 metres above sea level, and that at current rates of sea level rise, significant portions of the country will be periodically uninhabitable within a generation.</p><p>Tuvalu is not alone. Kiribati, whose 33 atolls span an area larger than India\'s exclusive economic zone, has purchased 20 square kilometres of land in Fiji as what its government describes as a lifeboat — a physical location to which its people might eventually relocate. The Marshall Islands has pursued a legal strategy, arguing before international bodies that its nationhood and maritime rights should persist even as its physical territory submerges.</p>','cat'=>'World News','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1507525428034-b723cf961d3e?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Climate','Pacific','Sea Level','Nations'],'date'=>'2024-04-15 16:30:00'],
        ['id'=>9120,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>445,'title'=>'How Formula 1 Became the World\'s Most Watched Sport','exc'=>'A decade ago, F1 was an ageing sport with declining audiences. Netflix changed everything.','body'=>'<p>In 2017, Formula 1 was sold by its longtime owner Bernie Ecclestone to Liberty Media for $8 billion — a transaction widely understood to represent the beginning of a turnaround strategy. The sport\'s global television audience had been declining for a decade. Its demographic was ageing. Its venues were contracting. Its broadcast deals were structured around pay-television models that limited penetration in key growth markets.</p><p>The turnaround, when it came, arrived from an unexpected direction. Drive to Survive, the Netflix documentary series launched in 2019, exposed the sport to an audience that had no particular interest in motor racing and found, to general surprise, that it was gripped. The series was not primarily about racing — it was about personalities, rivalries, team politics, and the human drama of extreme competition. It was soap opera in engineering overalls, and it worked.</p>','cat'=>'Sports','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1558618666-fcd25c85cd64?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Formula 1','Sports','Netflix','Media'],'date'=>'2022-03-27 10:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9121,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>319,'title'=>'The Neuroscience of Bad Habits (and How to Break Them)','exc'=>'Habits are encoded in a part of the brain that is largely immune to reason. Understanding the mechanism is the first step to changing it.','body'=>'<p>The basal ganglia, a cluster of nuclei deep in the brain\'s subcortex, do not think in the way that the prefrontal cortex thinks. They do not reason, weigh options, or consult long-term goals. They recognise patterns and trigger responses with an efficiency and speed that conscious deliberation cannot match. And it is here, in this ancient and largely unconscious system, that habits live.</p><p>Understanding habit formation at a neurological level has practical implications that researchers have only begun to fully map. The key insight is that habits are never truly erased — the neural pathways that underpin them remain intact even after years of disuse. What changes when a habit is successfully broken is not the pathway itself but the relative strength of a competing pathway. The old habit waits, dormant, requiring only the original cue to reactivate.</p>','cat'=>'Science','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Neuroscience','Psychology','Health'],'date'=>'2020-11-12 13:30:00'],
        ['id'=>9122,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>396,'title'=>'The Billion-Dollar Business of Competitive Eating','exc'=>'What began as small-town fair entertainment now generates eight-figure revenues. Meet the people who made overeating a spectator sport.','body'=>'<p>The Nathan\'s Famous Hot Dog Eating Contest, held annually at Coney Island on the Fourth of July, draws a crowd of tens of thousands and a television audience of several million. It is the centrepiece of an industry that did not exist as a recognisable commercial enterprise twenty-five years ago and now generates revenues that analysts estimate at close to half a billion dollars globally when sponsorship, media rights, event fees, and merchandise are included.</p><p>Major League Eating, the governing body of professional competitive eating in North America, sanctions more than 80 events a year and maintains a ranked roster of professional athletes — the preferred term in the industry — who travel from contest to contest collecting prize money, appearance fees, and sponsorship deals from hot sauce companies, food brands, and energy drink manufacturers.</p>','cat'=>'Entertainment','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1555396273-367ea4eb4db5?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Food','Sports','Business','Entertainment'],'date'=>'2018-08-16 15:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9123,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>241,'title'=>'The Dark Web\'s Most Legitimate Use Case: Journalism','exc'=>'Reporters and sources in authoritarian countries increasingly rely on Tor and encrypted dead-drops to communicate. The tools are working.','body'=>'<p>In countries where journalists can be prosecuted for their sources, the question of how to receive sensitive information securely is not theoretical. It is the difference between a story that gets published and a source that goes to prison. For a growing number of investigative journalists working in authoritarian environments — and, increasingly, in democracies with aggressive media law enforcement — the answer involves tools originally developed for anonymous internet browsing and later adapted for secure communication.</p><p>SecureDrop, an open-source platform developed by the Freedom of the Press Foundation, allows sources to submit documents and messages to news organisations through the Tor network, which routes communications through multiple layers of encrypted relays to obscure the origin. The platform is now used by more than 70 media organisations including The New York Times, The Washington Post, and Der Spiegel.</p>','cat'=>'Technology','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Journalism','Privacy','Technology','Security'],'date'=>'2019-04-05 09:45:00'],
        ['id'=>9124,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>488,'title'=>'The Countries Winning the Brain Drain War','exc'=>'Germany, Canada, and the UAE are deploying increasingly sophisticated strategies to attract skilled workers. The losers are losing badly.','body'=>'<p>In the summer of 2022, Germany passed the Skilled Workers Immigration Act — a landmark reform that made it substantially easier for qualified workers from outside the European Union to obtain residence permits, have foreign qualifications recognised, and bring their families. The legislation was the culmination of a decade of debate about how to address a structural labour shortage that economists projected would leave the country with a deficit of three million skilled workers by 2030.</p><p>Germany was not alone. Canada\'s Express Entry system, which uses a points-based algorithm to identify and fast-track high-skilled immigrants, has been refined continuously since its launch in 2015 and now processes over 100,000 applications annually. The UAE has introduced a Golden Visa programme offering ten-year residence to investors, entrepreneurs, researchers, and specialists. Australia has launched a National Innovation Visa. The competition for talent has become as fierce as the competition for capital.</p>','cat'=>'World News','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1529108190281-9a4f620bc2d8?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Immigration','Economy','Workforce'],'date'=>'2023-02-14 11:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9125,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>312,'title'=>'What the World Gets Wrong About Inflation','exc'=>'Public understanding of inflation is shaped by headlines, not economics. The gap between what people believe and what the data shows has real consequences.','body'=>'<p>Ask a sample of people to define inflation and most will say something like "prices going up." This is not wrong, but it misses something important. Inflation is not any price going up — it is a broad, sustained increase in the general price level, typically measured by tracking the cost of a representative basket of goods and services over time. The distinction matters enormously for policy, and the gap between popular understanding and economic reality has practical consequences for democratic accountability of central banks.</p><p>One common misconception is that inflation primarily benefits debtors at the expense of creditors — that it is, in effect, a tax on savers. This is partially true in real terms: a fixed debt becomes smaller in real value as prices rise, and cash savings lose purchasing power. But the picture is complicated by the fact that nominal interest rates tend to rise with inflation, and by the distributional effects of inflation, which fall disproportionately on households with lower incomes because they spend a larger share of earnings on necessities.</p>','cat'=>'Business','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Economics','Inflation','Finance'],'date'=>'2022-07-08 09:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9126,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>267,'title'=>'The Surgeon Who Operates With a Joystick','exc'=>'Robotic surgery is becoming standard in major hospitals worldwide. Patients do better. So why are doctors still debating it?','body'=>'<p>The da Vinci surgical system looks, from the outside, like a science fiction prop — a cluster of articulated arms mounted on a wheeled tower, manipulating instruments inside a patient\'s body while a surgeon sits at a console across the room, hands on controls that feel, they say, something like a video game. The image troubles some people. It troubles, in particular, some surgeons, who worry about the loss of haptic feedback, the dependence on technology that can fail, and the risk of de-skilling a generation of physicians who may never develop the manual dexterity of their predecessors.</p><p>The clinical evidence, however, is increasingly difficult to argue with. Meta-analyses of robotic-assisted surgery consistently show reductions in blood loss, shorter hospital stays, lower rates of complication, and faster recovery times compared to conventional laparoscopic and open approaches in a range of procedures. The da Vinci system has now been used in more than 10 million operations worldwide.</p>','cat'=>'Science','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1576091160550-2173dba999ef?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Medicine','Robotics','Technology','Surgery'],'date'=>'2020-05-28 14:15:00'],
        ['id'=>9127,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>534,'title'=>'The Rise of the 15-Minute City','exc'=>'Paris, Melbourne, and Portland are redesigning their urban fabric so residents can meet all basic needs within a short walk or cycle. Critics call it social engineering.','body'=>'<p>The concept is deceptively simple: design cities so that everything a resident needs on a daily basis — work, shopping, healthcare, education, leisure, and green space — is accessible within a 15-minute walk or cycle from their home. The idea, developed by French-Colombian scientist Carlos Moreno and adopted as a planning framework by Paris under Mayor Anne Hidalgo, has spread from urban planning seminars to mainstream policy debate with unusual speed, and with it has come an unusual quantity of controversy.</p><p>The technical case for 15-minute city planning is well-established. Studies consistently show that urban residents in walkable, mixed-use neighbourhoods generate significantly lower transport emissions, exhibit better health outcomes, report higher levels of social connection, and are less economically vulnerable to fuel price shocks than their counterparts in car-dependent suburbs. The environmental and public health arguments are largely uncontested.</p>','cat'=>'World News','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1534430480872-3498386e7856?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Urban Planning','Cities','Transport','Environment'],'date'=>'2023-10-30 10:30:00'],
        ['id'=>9128,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>298,'title'=>'The Gold Rush Beneath the Ocean','exc'=>'Deep-sea mining could unlock trillions of dollars in minerals critical for the energy transition. It could also destroy ecosystems we barely understand.','body'=>'<p>At depths of 4,000 to 6,000 metres on the floor of the Pacific Ocean, in an area known as the Clarion-Clipperton Zone, lies one of the largest untapped mineral deposits on earth. The nodules — fist-sized lumps of manganese, cobalt, copper, and nickel that have been accumulating on the seabed for millions of years — contain concentrations of battery metals that exceed the world\'s known terrestrial reserves. They have attracted the attention of mining companies and the governments of countries hoping to secure strategic mineral supplies for the clean energy transition.</p><p>They have also attracted the attention of marine biologists, who have documented ecosystems of surprising richness and strangeness in what was long assumed to be a barren abyss. Species found in the Clarion-Clipperton Zone include animals that live for centuries or millennia, organisms that have never been described by science, and food webs that depend on the nodules themselves as habitat substrate. Disturb the nodules and you disturb communities that took geological time to form.</p>','cat'=>'Science','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1507525428034-b723cf961d3e?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Mining','Ocean','Environment','Minerals'],'date'=>'2024-05-22 15:45:00'],
        ['id'=>9129,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>178,'title'=>'The Art Market\'s Dirty Secret','exc'=>'Billions in art change hands every year with almost no regulatory oversight. Money laundering experts say the sector is uniquely vulnerable.','body'=>'<p>In 2017, a painting attributed to Leonardo da Vinci sold at Christie\'s in New York for $450.3 million, the highest price ever paid for an artwork at auction. The buyer was not publicly identified. The seller was not publicly identified. The provenance of the painting — the chain of ownership that traces where it has been and who has owned it — contained significant gaps. Under the laws then in force in New York, there was no legal obligation to identify either party or to conduct any form of due diligence on the source of the funds.</p><p>This arrangement, which would be impossible in almost any other high-value asset class, has made the global art market one of the least regulated major financial sectors in the world, and, critics argue, one of the most vulnerable to money laundering, sanctions evasion, and tax fraud. The United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime estimates that art and antiquities trafficking is the third-largest black market globally, after drugs and arms.</p>','cat'=>'Business','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Art','Finance','Money Laundering','Regulation'],'date'=>'2018-11-03 13:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9130,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>423,'title'=>'The Unlikely Comeback of Nuclear Power','exc'=>'After decades of decline following Three Mile Island and Fukushima, nuclear is experiencing a renaissance driven by climate targets and AI energy demand.','body'=>'<p>In April 2023, the last nuclear reactor at Germany\'s Isar 2 plant was shut down, completing the country\'s Atomausstieg — the nuclear exit mandated by law following the Fukushima disaster. It was widely reported as the final nail in the coffin of an industry that had been in retreat for forty years. Within eighteen months, it was looking less like a final chapter and more like an outlier.</p><p>By the end of 2024, more new nuclear capacity was under construction globally than at any point since the late 1980s. South Korea reversed its nuclear phase-out. Poland broke ground on its first reactor. France accelerated its programme of new builds. Japan restarted plants shuttered after Fukushima. The United States extended the licences of reactors scheduled for retirement and fast-tracked new small modular reactor designs. Even Germany began to discuss whether its decision had been premature.</p>','cat'=>'Science','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1473341304170-971dccb5ac1e?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Nuclear','Energy','Climate','Technology'],'date'=>'2024-09-11 10:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9131,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>267,'title'=>'The Countries That Run on Referendums','exc'=>'Switzerland holds national votes several times a year on everything from tax policy to migration. What other democracies can learn from direct democracy\'s most committed practitioner.','body'=>'<p>On a typical Sunday in Switzerland, citizens across the country\'s 26 cantons may be voting simultaneously on half a dozen separate propositions — federal laws, constitutional amendments, cantonal initiatives, local referendums. The Swiss hold four national vote days a year, each addressing between three and six separate questions, and the system has operated more or less continuously since 1848. By any measure, Switzerland conducts more direct democratic exercises than any other nation on earth.</p><p>The model has its critics, including within Switzerland. Referendums have been used to pass measures that restrict immigration, limit minarets, and approve the mass internment of foreign nationals convicted of minor offences — decisions that Swiss courts have subsequently found to be in tension with constitutional rights and international human rights obligations. The same mechanism that produced some of the world\'s strongest privacy protections and banking regulations has also delivered outcomes that embarrass a country that sees itself as a bastion of liberal values.</p>','cat'=>'Politics','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Democracy','Switzerland','Politics','Governance'],'date'=>'2019-10-17 11:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9132,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>351,'title'=>'The Women Running the World\'s Most Dangerous Prisons','exc'=>'In several Central American countries, female guards have been placed in charge of maximum-security male facilities, with striking results.','body'=>'<p>The Honduran prison known as La Tolva sits in a scrubby valley outside the city of Morocelí and houses some of the country\'s most dangerous gang members, including senior figures from MS-13 and Barrio 18. It is also, since 2022, managed entirely by female staff. The switch was not a social experiment but a response to a crisis: the previous male-run administration had allowed the facility to become effectively gang-controlled, with inmates directing criminal operations from their cells, overseeing drug trafficking, and ordering killings outside the prison walls.</p><p>Honduras is not alone. El Salvador\'s mega-prison, opened in 2023 to house 40,000 gang members arrested under President Bukele\'s state of emergency, is administered by a female superintendent. Guatemala has placed women in charge of several of its most troubled male facilities. The results, researchers cautiously note, have been mixed but on average positive: incident rates fall, corruption drops, and the dynamics of intimidation that allow gang hierarchies to operate inside prisons are disrupted.</p>','cat'=>'World News','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1529107386315-e1a2ed48a620?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Crime','Prisons','Central America','Society'],'date'=>'2023-05-08 14:30:00'],
        ['id'=>9133,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>445,'title'=>'Inside the Global Scramble for Critical Minerals','exc'=>'Lithium, cobalt, and rare earths are the new oil, and the countries that control them are rewriting the rules of geopolitics.','body'=>'<p>The Democratic Republic of Congo produces roughly 70% of the world\'s cobalt. Chile and Australia together account for more than half of global lithium extraction. China controls more than 80% of the processing capacity for rare earth elements, the seventeen metals whose magnetic, optical, and catalytic properties make them essential for electric vehicles, wind turbines, smartphones, and advanced weapons systems. These concentration ratios, in an era of accelerating electrification, have transformed what were previously specialist industrial supply chains into strategic national security concerns.</p><p>The realisation has arrived with particular urgency in Washington, Brussels, and Tokyo. The energy transition requires enormous quantities of these materials — estimates suggest that meeting 2050 net-zero targets will require a tenfold increase in the supply of minerals including lithium, cobalt, nickel, and manganese — and much of that supply currently flows through geopolitical rivals or through conflict zones with limited rule of law.</p>','cat'=>'Business','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1526304640581-d334cdbbf45e?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Minerals','Geopolitics','Energy','Supply Chain'],'date'=>'2022-06-15 09:30:00'],
        ['id'=>9134,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>212,'title'=>'The Men Who Swim the World\'s Wildest Rivers','exc'=>'A small community of extreme swimmers are completing multi-week descents of rivers through some of the planet\'s most remote terrain. Here\'s why.','body'=>'<p>The Congo River begins its 4,700-kilometre journey in the highlands of Zambia and descends to the Atlantic through the world\'s second-largest rainforest, passing through cataracts, gorges, and stretches of whitewater that rank among the most technically demanding in the world. In 2023, a team of four swimmers became the first people to traverse its entire length by swimming — a 68-day journey that required navigating crocodile habitat, negotiating permissions from armed groups who control sections of the river corridor, and surviving a stretch near the equator where the river\'s surface current runs at 7 metres per second.</p><p>River swimming at this scale is a marginal pursuit even within the world of extreme sports, and the community of people who practise it at the highest level is small enough that most practitioners know each other. What unites them, beyond a tolerance for cold water and uncertain conditions, is a philosophical commitment to engaging with landscapes at a pace and intimacy that no other form of travel allows.</p>','cat'=>'Lifestyle','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1500534314209-a25ddb2bd429?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Adventure','Swimming','Exploration'],'date'=>'2024-08-19 15:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9135,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>378,'title'=>'The Algorithm That Decides Who Gets a Mortgage','exc'=>'Automated underwriting systems now approve or deny the majority of home loan applications in the US. Critics say they perpetuate racial disparities built into historical data.','body'=>'<p>In the United States, more than 80% of conventional mortgage applications are now initially evaluated by automated underwriting systems — algorithms that assess creditworthiness by analysing hundreds of variables in a matter of seconds and generating a recommendation of approve, refer, or deny. The two dominant systems, Fannie Mae\'s Desktop Underwriter and Freddie Mac\'s Loan Product Advisor, together process more than ten million applications annually and shape the mortgage decisions made by virtually every major lender in the country.</p><p>Proponents argue that automated underwriting has made the mortgage market more consistent and less susceptible to the individual biases of human loan officers. Critics argue that the algorithms have embedded historical patterns of racial discrimination into a seemingly neutral technical process, because the training data reflects decades of lending practices that systematically denied mortgages to Black and Hispanic applicants in redlined neighbourhoods.</p>','cat'=>'Technology','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['AI','Housing','Race','Finance'],'date'=>'2021-12-07 10:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9136,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>489,'title'=>'The World\'s Most Expensive Cities: A New Ranking Upends Old Assumptions','exc'=>'Zurich and Singapore have displaced New York and London at the top of the cost-of-living index, as remote work reshapes where the wealthy choose to live.','body'=>'<p>For most of the past two decades, New York and London competed for the distinction of being the world\'s most expensive city — a title that, depending on the methodology and currency fluctuations, they traded back and forth with Hong Kong, Tokyo, and Zurich. The 2024 edition of the Economist Intelligence Unit\'s Worldwide Cost of Living Index disrupted that established hierarchy: Zurich and Singapore now occupy the top two positions, with New York falling to sixth and London not appearing until fourteenth.</p><p>The shift reflects changes that go beyond exchange rates. Zurich has seen a surge in demand from tech executives and finance professionals seeking proximity to European headquarters without subjecting themselves to the tax regimes of France, Germany, or the United Kingdom. Singapore has benefited from the massive repatriation of capital and talent from Hong Kong following the imposition of the National Security Law in 2020.</p>','cat'=>'Business','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1516738901171-8eb4fc13bd20?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Cities','Cost of Living','Economics','Lifestyle'],'date'=>'2024-07-30 13:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9137,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>312,'title'=>'The Last Typewriter Repairman in Manhattan','exc'=>'At 78, Mannie Jacobson still fixes typewriters six days a week in a shop his father opened in 1951. He has more customers than ever.','body'=>'<p>The shop is easy to miss. It occupies thirty feet of frontage on West 45th Street between a chain pharmacy and a food delivery pickup hub, and the sign above the door — Jacobson\'s Typewriter Service — has not been updated since 1973. The window display features a 1962 IBM Selectric, an Olivetti Lettera 32, and a handwritten card advertising full restoration services starting at $65. Inside, behind a glass counter loaded with orphaned parts and spools of correction tape, sits Mannie Jacobson, whose hands have been inside typewriters for sixty years.</p><p>Jacobson learned the trade from his father, who learned it from a German mechanic who emigrated after the war. He can identify a machine\'s model and manufacturing year by the sound it makes before he has looked at it, and he maintains a client list that now includes a waiting roster — something that was not true ten years ago.</p>','cat'=>'Lifestyle','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1507003211169-0a1dd7228f2d?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Craft','New York','Nostalgia','Technology'],'date'=>'2018-04-21 10:30:00'],
        ['id'=>9138,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>267,'title'=>'The Countries Experimenting With a Universal Basic Income','exc'=>'Pilots in Finland, Kenya, and Stockton, California have produced nuanced results that resist simple political narratives.','body'=>'<p>The idea of a universal basic income — a regular, unconditional cash payment to every citizen regardless of employment status — has been periodically revived and dismissed for more than two centuries, from Thomas Paine\'s proposal for a capital grant to every adult in the 1790s to Milton Friedman\'s negative income tax proposal in the 1960s to the guaranteed annual income experiments of the 1970s. What makes the current moment different is the quality and quantity of empirical evidence now available from real-world pilots conducted across a diverse range of economic and cultural contexts.</p><p>Finland\'s two-year experiment, concluded in 2018, gave 2,000 randomly selected unemployed people a monthly payment of €560 with no strings attached. The results were more positive than critics had predicted and more modest than advocates had hoped: recipients reported higher levels of wellbeing, lower stress, and greater trust in public institutions, but employment rates were not significantly higher than the control group and the fiscal cost of a universal rollout was eye-watering.</p>','cat'=>'Politics','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Economics','UBI','Policy','Society'],'date'=>'2021-01-19 09:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9139,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>534,'title'=>'Africa\'s Youngest Population Is Its Greatest Asset — and Its Greatest Challenge','exc'=>'By 2050, Africa will be home to 2.5 billion people, most of them under 30. Whether this is a demographic dividend or a crisis depends almost entirely on education and jobs.','body'=>'<p>The median age in Niger is 14.8 years. In Uganda, it is 16.4. In Mali, 16.9. These numbers represent a demographic reality without precedent in human history: a continent where more than half the population is under 20, where the working-age cohort is growing by 15 million people every year, and where the labour markets that will need to absorb these young people currently generate fewer than three million formal sector jobs annually.</p><p>The gap between people entering working age and jobs available to them is the central economic and political challenge facing sub-Saharan Africa in the coming decades, and it is a challenge with no clean solution. Creating the number of jobs required demands sustained GDP growth rates of 8% or more for decades — rates that virtually no country has maintained for more than a generation and that require simultaneous improvements in infrastructure, governance, education quality, access to capital, and regional market integration.</p>','cat'=>'World News','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1529107386315-e1a2ed48a620?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Africa','Demographics','Economy','Development'],'date'=>'2022-10-04 14:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9140,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>298,'title'=>'The Scientist Trying to Bring Back the Woolly Mammoth','exc'=>'Colossal Biosciences is editing the genome of Asian elephants to restore mammoth traits. Critics question whether the goal is science or spectacle.','body'=>'<p>Ben Novak first became obsessed with extinct animals as a child, when he read about the passenger pigeon and could not understand why a bird that once migrated in flocks of billions — flocks so dense they darkened the sky for hours — no longer existed. He is now 36, holds a research position at a university, and spends most of his working hours attempting to reverse that extinction using CRISPR gene editing and stem cell technology. He is one of a small but growing community of scientists and entrepreneurs pursuing what has come to be known as de-extinction.</p><p>Colossal Biosciences, the company that has attracted the most attention and the most funding, is pursuing two headline projects simultaneously: the restoration of the woolly mammoth and the thylacine, also known as the Tasmanian tiger, which went extinct in 1936. The company\'s approach involves editing the genome of the closest living relative — the Asian elephant in the mammoth\'s case, the fat-tailed dunnart in the thylacine\'s — to incorporate the genes responsible for the extinct species\' distinctive traits.</p>','cat'=>'Science','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1584308666744-24d5c474f2ae?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Science','Genetics','Extinction','Technology'],'date'=>'2023-03-15 11:30:00'],
        ['id'=>9141,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>423,'title'=>'The Billion-Dollar Business of Competitive Gaming','exc'=>'Esports has grown from basement hobby to stadium spectacle to investment bust — and might be on its way back. We track the money.','body'=>'<p>In 2021, venture capital and media investment into esports exceeded $1.5 billion, with companies and funds betting that competitive gaming would follow the same trajectory as traditional sports leagues and produce stable, scalable revenues from media rights, sponsorship, and merchandise. The investment thesis was straightforward: esports attracted young audiences that conventional sports media could not reach, and those audiences were growing globally.</p><p>Three years later, several prominent esports organisations had filed for bankruptcy, media rights deals had collapsed, and the promised revenues had failed to materialise at anything close to the projected scale. The story is more complicated than the obituaries suggested, however: while the investment bubble burst with predictable brutality, the underlying activity — competitive gaming — continued to grow. League of Legends Worlds 2023 attracted 73 million peak viewers. The ESL Pro League has maintained consistent audiences. Valorant Premier drew millions of viewers across multiple platforms.</p>','cat'=>'Technology','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1542751371-adc38448a05e?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Esports','Gaming','Business','Entertainment'],'date'=>'2024-01-07 16:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9142,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>178,'title'=>'The Art of the Apology: Why Some Work and Others Make Things Worse','exc'=>'Research on effective apologies reveals that most public apologies fail on their most basic function. Here\'s the science of saying sorry well.','body'=>'<p>In 2010, British Petroleum CEO Tony Hayward appeared at a congressional hearing in the aftermath of the Deepwater Horizon disaster and told reporters: "I\'d like my life back." The statement, expressing personal inconvenience in the context of an environmental catastrophe that had killed eleven workers and was coating hundreds of miles of Gulf Coast in crude oil, became a masterclass in how not to apologise. Hayward was replaced as CEO six weeks later.</p><p>What makes an apology effective is a question that researchers in psychology, communication, and organisational behaviour have studied with considerable rigour. The findings are consistent: effective apologies require specific acknowledgement of the harm caused, expression of genuine remorse, an explanation (not an excuse) for why the harm occurred, and a credible commitment to changed behaviour. Missing or botching any of these components reduces the apology\'s effectiveness dramatically — and in some cases makes the original offence worse.</p>','cat'=>'Lifestyle','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Psychology','Communication','Society'],'date'=>'2018-06-11 10:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9143,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>489,'title'=>'The Rise of the Platform Cooperative','exc'=>'Worker-owned alternatives to Uber, Airbnb, and Amazon have launched in dozens of cities. Some are thriving. Here\'s how they\'re doing it.','body'=>'<p>In Denver, Colorado, a platform called Up&Go provides home cleaning services through an app that looks, to the customer, almost identical to comparable gig economy platforms. The workers are assigned jobs, receive ratings, and communicate through the app. The difference is that the workers own the platform, set their own wages, and receive the overwhelming majority of revenue from each booking rather than a fraction. Up&Go takes 5% to cover running costs. By comparison, Handy and TaskRabbit take between 20% and 30%.</p><p>Up&Go is one of a growing number of platform cooperatives — worker-owned digital businesses structured as cooperatives rather than investor-owned corporations. The model has been proposed by labour economists and cooperative advocates for as long as the gig economy has existed, but it has taken until relatively recently for enough examples to accumulate to allow serious assessment of whether it is viable at scale.</p>','cat'=>'Business','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1497366216548-37526070297c?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Cooperatives','Gig Economy','Business','Labour'],'date'=>'2022-04-27 13:30:00'],
        ['id'=>9144,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>312,'title'=>'The Marathon Monks of Mount Hiei','exc'=>'For 1,200 years, an order of Japanese Buddhist monks has practised a seven-year walking meditation that includes covering 84 kilometres in a day. Some complete it. Some die.','body'=>'<p>The practice is called kaihōgyō — circling the mountain — and it has been conducted, with brief interruptions for war and pandemic, at Mount Hiei in Japan for over twelve centuries. The practitioners are monks of the Tendai sect, and they walk. Each day during the first three years of the practice, they rise at midnight, don white robes and straw sandals, and walk a circuit of approximately 30 kilometres through the mountain\'s temple complex, stopping at over 250 stations to chant sutras. In the fourth and fifth years, the daily circuit extends to 60 kilometres. In the seventh year, it reaches 84 kilometres.</p><p>The seven-year discipline, known as sennichi kaihōgyō, is considered one of the most demanding physical and spiritual practices in any living religious tradition. Roughly 50 monks have completed it in the past century. Those who attempt it and find themselves unable to continue before the first three-year commitment is finished are expected to take their own lives. Some have.</p>','cat'=>'World News','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1545558014-8692077e9b5c?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Japan','Buddhism','Religion','Endurance'],'date'=>'2019-01-15 09:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9145,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>267,'title'=>'The Countries That Abolished Their Militaries (And What Happened)','exc'=>'Costa Rica, Iceland, and 20 other nations have no standing armies. Their security arrangements are more complicated than the headlines suggest.','body'=>'<p>Costa Rica abolished its military in 1948, and the decision — enshrined in a constitution that prohibits the raising of an army in perpetuity — has become a point of national identity and international pride. The story is frequently cited as proof that small nations can achieve security without armed forces, that the resources saved can be redirected to education and healthcare, and that pacifism is a viable state policy rather than a utopian fantasy.</p><p>The reality is more nuanced. Costa Rica\'s security is underpinned by geography, by US strategic interests in the region, and by a regional security architecture that includes the Organisation of American States, whose mutual defence commitments mean that an attack on Costa Rica would be treated as an attack on its member states. The country does maintain a police force with paramilitary capabilities, border security operations, and a Coast Guard. It is not, in practice, defenceless; it has simply transferred the functions of a military to other institutions.</p>','cat'=>'Politics','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Military','Security','Policy','Countries'],'date'=>'2020-08-04 11:30:00'],
        ['id'=>9146,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>398,'title'=>'The Science of Sleep (and Why We\'re Getting Less of It)','exc'=>'Humans sleep less than any other primate. Sleep deprivation is now a public health crisis. Here\'s what the research says about why — and what to do about it.','body'=>'<p>Matthew Walker\'s 2017 book Why We Sleep carried a stark opening message: sleep loss is "the greatest public health challenge we face in the 21st century." The claim was not unique to Walker — sleep researchers had been publishing on the health consequences of chronic sleep deprivation for decades — but the book\'s commercial success brought the message to a mainstream audience and helped crystallise a conversation about a behaviour that most people treat as a lifestyle choice rather than a physiological necessity.</p><p>The evidence on the consequences of insufficient sleep is extensive and sobering. Chronic sleep restriction — defined as sleeping less than seven hours a night for extended periods — is associated with increased risk of cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes, obesity, depression, anxiety disorders, reduced immune function, and cognitive decline. A single night of sleep deprivation produces impairments in cognitive performance equivalent to a blood alcohol level above the legal driving limit in most jurisdictions.</p>','cat'=>'Science','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1512621776951-a57141f2eefd?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Sleep','Health','Science','Wellbeing'],'date'=>'2023-09-20 14:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9147,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>534,'title'=>'The Cities Being Swallowed by the Sea','exc'=>'Jakarta, Bangkok, and New Orleans are sinking faster than sea levels are rising. The engineering solutions are extraordinary. They might not be enough.','body'=>'<p>Jakarta is sinking at a rate of between 1 and 25 centimetres per year, depending on the district, making it one of the fastest-subsiding cities on earth. The primary driver is groundwater extraction: Jakarta sits on loose sediment that compacts as water is pumped from the aquifers below, and decades of inadequate piped water infrastructure have forced residents and businesses to drill their own wells. The result is a city where large sections of the northern coastal zone now sit below sea level, defended from flooding only by a network of aging sea walls and pump stations.</p><p>The Indonesian government announced in 2019 that it would relocate the national capital to a new city called Nusantara, on the island of Borneo — in part because Jakarta was becoming literally ungovernable in the face of its subsidence, flooding, and infrastructure crises. Construction of Nusantara began in 2022. Jakarta, with its population of 10 million, is not being abandoned; the question is what it will look like in 50 years.</p>','cat'=>'World News','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1569144157591-c60f3f82f137?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Climate','Cities','Sea Level','Infrastructure'],'date'=>'2024-02-05 09:30:00'],
        ['id'=>9148,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>312,'title'=>'What the Failure of Crypto Winter Taught Us','exc'=>'The 2022 collapse wiped out $2 trillion in market value. Three years later, some things are clearer — and some are not.','body'=>'<p>In November 2022, FTX, the third-largest cryptocurrency exchange in the world by volume, filed for bankruptcy following the revelation that it had used customer deposits to fund the trading activity of its sister hedge fund, Alameda Research. The collapse was the most dramatic single event in a year of cascading failures that together erased approximately $2 trillion from the market capitalisation of the crypto ecosystem — an implosion comparable in speed and scale to the dot-com bust of 2000 and the 2008 financial crisis.</p><p>Three years on, the cryptocurrency market has recovered sufficiently that Bitcoin returned to all-time highs in early 2024, following the approval of spot Bitcoin exchange-traded funds by US regulators. The recovery is real. But the question of what exactly the collapse of 2022 demonstrated about the nature of the asset class, its governance, and its long-term viability as a financial infrastructure remains genuinely contested.</p>','cat'=>'Business','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Crypto','Finance','FTX','Technology'],'date'=>'2025-01-14 10:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9149,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>267,'title'=>'The Craft Beer Revolution: What Happened After the Boom?','exc'=>'At peak, the US had over 9,000 craft breweries. Now hundreds are closing every year. What went wrong — and what\'s surviving?','body'=>'<p>The American craft beer industry peaked, by most measures, around 2019. At that point, more than 8,000 breweries were operating across the country, up from fewer than 1,000 in 2000. The expansion had been driven by a consumer appetite for variety, local provenance, and flavour complexity that the major beer companies had spent decades ignoring. Craft brewing was genuinely transformative: it restored regional identity to an industry that had been homogenised by decades of consolidation, produced beers of remarkable quality and diversity, and created tens of thousands of small businesses in communities that had lost their manufacturing base.</p><p>Then it got complicated. Post-pandemic, brewery closures began outpacing openings for the first time. Established craft brands found themselves squeezed between consumers\' growing interest in hard seltzer, non-alcoholic beer, and cannabis-infused beverages on one side, and the aggressive acquisition strategies of multinational beer companies on the other. The economics that had made small-scale brewing viable — tap room revenues, local distribution advantages, consumer willingness to pay premium prices for local — began to shift.</p>','cat'=>'Lifestyle','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1555396273-367ea4eb4db5?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Beer','Business','Food','Culture'],'date'=>'2024-10-28 11:00:00'],
        ['id'=>9150,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>489,'title'=>'The Quiet Epidemic of Long-Term Sick Leave','exc'=>'In wealthy countries, the number of working-age adults on long-term disability benefits has risen sharply since 2020. No one is quite sure why.','body'=>'<p>In the United Kingdom, the number of working-age people classified as economically inactive due to long-term illness rose by approximately 500,000 between 2019 and 2023, to reach a total of 2.6 million. Similar patterns have emerged in the United States, where the Social Security Administration has seen increased applications for disability benefits, in Sweden, in the Netherlands, and in Australia. The scale and cross-national consistency of the increase suggests it is not a statistical artefact.</p><p>The causes are contested. The most commonly cited explanation is long COVID — the persistent symptoms that affect a significant minority of people following SARS-CoV-2 infection. Estimates of the number of people with functionally impairing long COVID vary widely, but the Office for National Statistics put the UK figure at 1.9 million in mid-2023, with approximately 400,000 reporting significant impairment to daily activities. If a fraction of this population has been unable to maintain employment, long COVID alone could account for a large part of the increase.</p>','cat'=>'Science','author'=>'Chris Morgan','img'=>'','tags'=>['Health','Economy','Disability','Society'],'date'=>'2023-12-14 13:00:00'],
    ];
}

// ── Helper: normalize DB row to frontend format ───────────
function formatArticle(array $row): array {
    return [
        'id'        => (int)$row['id'],
        'title'     => $row['title']    ?? '',
        'exc'       => $row['excerpt']  ?? '',
        'body'      => $row['content']  ?? '',
        'cat'       => $row['category'] ?? 'World News',
        'author'    => $row['author']   ?? 'Staff Reporter',
        'img'       => $row['image']    ?? '',
        'tags'      => array_values(array_filter(array_map('trim', explode(',', $row['tags'] ?? '')))),
        'date'        => $row['date']        ?? date('Y-m-d H:i:s'),
        'last_edited' => $row['last_edited'] ?? null,
        'published'   => (bool)($row['published'] ?? true),   // ← fixed: use actual DB value
        'featured'    => (bool)$row['featured'],
        'breaking'    => (bool)$row['breaking'],
        'views'       => (int)$row['views'],
    ];
}
